Sval: The address bar lists its rows in fixed sections

The Launcher's rows come in sections after the row Enter takes: open tabs,
pins and Favorites, commands, history, then search suggestions, each under
a heading, with no more rows than its limit, in an order the user sets.
The muxer places rows by the layout `makeDefaultResultGroups` makes, and no
pref sets that layout, so `getResultGroups` hands Firefox's layout to
`SvalLauncherSections.resultGroups`, which builds the sections around it,
and adds the settings to its cache key. Sval's own rows name their groups
through `UrlbarUtils.getResultGroup`'s `result.group` branch, which its
comment gives as there for `test_suggestedIndexRelativeToGroup.js`; a rebase
that drops it sends those rows to the general group, which the layout
places in History.

Firefox draws a heading from a row's type and provider, so a history row
would read "Your History" and a suggestion's would name the engine.
`#rowLabel` asks `SvalLauncherSections` first: a row's own heading, as
Sval's rows carry one, else that of the section its group is in.

The muxer keeps one heuristic, so when Sval's open-tab match is the row
Enter takes, the autofilled site and the web search it displaced would not
show; it keeps one row per address, the one it reads first, so a history
row could stand for a Favorite or a page picked before; and Firefox's
open-tab row for a pin's page would drop the pin's row. The muxer sorts the
results `SvalLauncherSections.unsorted` hands it, which lists the displaced
heuristics as rows of the top area, puts input history before the rest, and
leaves those rows out.

